  <!-- AEO Answer Capsule -->
  <div class="aeo-capsule bg-light-bg border-l-4 border-gold rounded-lg p-6 mb-10">
    <p>
      A wastewater centrifuge is a continuous industrial separator that uses high centrifugal force to dewater sludge, thicken solids, and clarify contaminated water. Decanter centrifuges handle high-sludge wastewater from municipal, manure, mining, food, and industrial streams, while disc-stack centrifuges polish lower-solids water and oily wastewater by removing fine suspended particles, oil, water, and sludge.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-4">
      Dolphin Centrifuge is the independent specialist in Alfa Laval centrifuges, with remanufactured machines, turnkey systems, parts, and service built on the Alfa Laval experience of its founder, Sanjay Prabhu, since 1986. Every wastewater system on this page is built around a remanufactured Alfa Laval centrifuge: NX 314, NX 414, NX 416, and NX 418 decanters for sludge, and WHPX 407, WHPX 513, WSPX 307, and MOPX 213 disc stack machines for clarification and oily water. Dolphin is an independent remanufacturer and is not affiliated with Alfa Laval.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="bg-gold/10 border-l-4 border-gold rounded-r-xl p-5 my-8 not-prose">
    <p class="font-bold text-navy text-base mb-1">Indicative Pricing</p>
    <p class="text-sm text-text-light leading-relaxed mb-0">Systems with full stainless-steel wetted parts (piping, valves, tanks, and centrifuge) start around the mid-$60s, EXW Warren MI. The system is engineered and built in Michigan around a remanufactured Alfa Laval centrifuge, test-run before shipment, and backed by a 6-month mechanical warranty, US engineering support, on-site startup, and ongoing technical support.</p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="types-of-wastewater">Types of Wastewater</h2>

  <p>
    Wastewater is a generic term for water with solid or liquid contaminants. For example, municipal wastewater has organic sludge, and <strong>industrial wastewater</strong> carries industrial or inorganic sludge, etc.
  </p>
  <p>
    Generation in large volumes is the other aspect of wastewater; therefore, the separation equipment must process large amounts of contaminated fluid.
  </p>
  <p>
    Wastewater from the sources, as mentioned earlier, contains bulk sludge, which is easier to separate. On the other hand, wastewater from mines and oilfield drilling operations contains fine, suspended solids requiring smaller mesh filtration or higher g-force centrifuges.
  </p>
  <p>
    We also have <strong>industrial effluent water</strong>, which contains oil in addition to the solids or sludge. This application may need two different centrifuges to separate the sludge and the oil from the de-sludged water.
  </p>

  <p>
    <a href="/wastewater-centrifuges-types-applications-amp-photos-dolphin-centrifuge/">Wastewater Centrifuges &mdash; Types and Capacities (Full Article)</a>
  </p>

  <h2 id="separation-technologies">Separation Technologies for Wastewater</h2>

  <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-3 gap-8 items-start my-8">
    <div class="bg-warm-white rounded-xl border border-gray-100 shadow-sm p-4">
      <figure>
        <img
          src="/images/wastewater-centrifuge/sharples-p3000-wastewater-decanter-300x300.webp"
          alt="Sharples P3000 Wastewater Decanter"
          width="300"
          height="300"
          loading="lazy"
          class="img-cap-300"
        />
        <figcaption>Sharples P3000 - 40 GPM Wastewater Decanter</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
    <div class="md:col-span-2 prose-dolphin">
      <p>
        Filtration is not an option for this application, given the type and volume of wastewater that needs to be purified. Other separation technologies, such as belts and filter presses, have limited uses in wastewater separation duties. Again, these technologies have drawbacks, including labor and equipment reliability issues that limit their widespread use in wastewater treatment plants.
      </p>
      <p>
        Mechanical separation using a wastewater centrifuge has many advantages for continuously processing large quantities of wastewater. As with all technologies, centrifuges also have some drawbacks highlighted below.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>



  <h2 id="operation">Wastewater Centrifuge Operation</h2>

  <div class="bg-warm-white rounded-xl border border-gray-100 shadow-sm p-6 my-8">
    <figure>
      <img
        src="/images/shared/Dewatering-Centrifuge-Animation.gif"
        alt="Dewatering Centrifuge Operation Animation"
        width="533"
        height="188"
        loading="lazy"
        class="img-cap-600"
      />
      <figcaption>Operation Cycle: 3,157 Gs Separation</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </div>

  <p>
    A wastewater centrifuge is a separation machine that utilizes a centrifugal force of <strong>3,157 Gs</strong> or higher to separate the wastewater from sludge. The sludge accumulates on the bowl periphery, and the internal auger scrapes towards the sludge discharge ports.
  </p>
  <p>
    Simultaneously, the clarified water flows out through the liquid outlet at the opposite end of the bowl. This process happens continuously, allowing for high-volume throughput without the downtime required for filter cleaning or media replacement.
  </p>

  <!-- Sanjay's dictation, tidied, approved 2026-11-27 -->
  <h2 id="bowl-and-conveyor">How the Bowl and the Conveyor Work Together</h2>

  <p>The bowl of the decanter rotates at a fixed speed, and the conveyor rotates at a differential speed through the gearbox. The gearbox creates a differential speed between the bowl and the conveyor.</p>

  <p>The centrifugal force pushes the solids, or the sludge, towards the periphery of the bowl, its internal surface, due to G-force.</p>

  <p>The conveyor, with the differential speed, is like a screw. It constantly pushes the accumulated sludge towards the tapered end, or the conical section, of the bowl, where there are outlet ports for the sludge to fall out.</p>

  <p>Because of the conical section, the liquid cannot get there due to the high G-force. Only the solids are pushed out. The sludge is pushed up along the so-called beach of the conical section, to eject out of the sludge discharge ports.</p>

  <p>The liquid, which is accumulating in the bowl, forms a pond, the so-called pond. It exits on the other side through weir plates, or weir dams, which control the depth of the pond inside the bowl. That is how the basic thing works.</p>

  <h3>Why the Scroll Turns at a Different Speed</h3>

  <p>The scroll turns at a slightly differential speed, as explained above, to push the solids towards the solids end while the bowl is fully rotating. The differential speed, like a screw, pushes the accumulated or separated solids up. It scrapes them, almost, from the bowl periphery towards the solids end, where eventually they eject out due to the high centrifugal force.</p>

  <h2 id="polymer">Polymer or Flocculant</h2>

  <p>For wastewater treatment, specifically for ETP and municipal wastewater, a polymer or flocculant is often added. The idea there is to consolidate, or precipitate, the fine suspended solids into bigger clumps of solids, to put it very loosely, so that they can be separated by the G-force of the decanter.</p>

  <p>The quantity of the flocculant, chemical or polymer to be used depends on the type of sludge, if it is activated, DAF or other types of sludge. It is best recommended by the flocculant or chemical supplier.</p>

  <h2 id="cake-dryness">How Dry the Sludge Cake Comes Out</h2>

  <p>The sludge cake comes out of the decanter with a consistency almost like fudge or toothpaste.</p>

  <p>The cake dryness can be controlled mainly by adjusting the pond depth. The secondary way to control it is by flow rate. The third way is by adjusting the auger differential speed.</p>

  <p>The percentage of solids is hard to estimate, but typically you can expect up to 50% solids or even higher.</p>

  <p>The cake can be runny and wet if the pond depth is deep. When the pond depth is shallow, the solids get drier and drier.</p>

  <h2 id="types">Types of Wastewater Centrifuges</h2>

  <p class="mb-8">
    Three types of centrifuges apply to wastewater depending on the type and amount of sludge. The following sections describe these broad categories for industrial and municipal use. For a detailed technical comparison, see our <a href="/difference-between-decanter-centrifuge-disc-centrifuge/">decanter vs disc stack centrifuge comparison</a>.
  </p>

  <h3 class="font-heading text-xl font-bold text-navy mb-4">Sludge Thickening or Sludge Dewatering Centrifuge</h3>
  <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-3 gap-8 items-start my-8">
    <div class="bg-warm-white rounded-xl border border-gray-100 shadow-sm p-4">
      <figure>
        <img
          src="/images/wastewater-centrifuge/Alfa-Laval-NX-314-Decanter-Centrifuge-600-1.webp"
          alt="Alfa Laval NX-314 Decanter Centrifuge"
          width="600"
          height="428"
          loading="lazy"
          class="img-cap-600"
        />
        <figcaption>Alfa Laval NX-314 Dewatering Decanter</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
    <div class="md:col-span-2 prose-dolphin">
      <p>
        A <a href="/dewatering-centrifuge/">sludge dewatering or sludge thickening centrifuge</a> is a <a href="/decanter-centrifuge/">decanter-type horizontal de-sludger</a> that can remove water from slurries to thicken the sludge. In other words, a heavy-duty industrial centrifuge dewaters and concentrates (thickens) the sludge, also known as a sludge thickening machine. These machines are available in different sizes to continuously process large quantities of sludge-laden water.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Livestock farm manure, municipal sewage, car washes, and oilfield drilling mud</strong> produce wastewater with high sludge content. Alfa Laval states that its decanter units can handle slurries with a solids content from as low as 0.1% w/w to more than 65% w/w. The primary objective of such applications is to reduce the sludge volume to minimize handling and disposal costs. The recovered water is often clean enough to be reused or discharged with minimal environmental impact.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h3 class="font-heading text-xl font-bold text-navy mb-4">Clarifying Centrifuge for Mining Effluent</h3>
  <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-3 gap-8 items-start my-8">
    <div class="bg-warm-white rounded-xl border border-gray-100 shadow-sm p-4">
      <figure>
        <img
          src="/images/wastewater-centrifuge/alfa-laval-wash-water-centrifuge-wspx-303.webp"
          alt="Alfa Laval WSPX-303 Concentrator Centrifuge"
          width="400"
          height="458"
          loading="lazy"
          class="img-cap-400"
        />
        <figcaption>Alfa Laval WSPX-303 Centrifuge</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
    <div class="md:col-span-2 prose-dolphin">
      <p>
        A wastewater centrifuge is a disc-stack type, a high-speed, self-cleaning separator that produces very high centrifugal forces that can separate micron-level suspended particles. The particles are pushed toward the bowl wall and automatically purged during the cleaning cycle. The cleared centrate discharges the bowl continuously through a centrally located outlet port.
      </p>
      <p>
        Wastewater from mining applications is one such example. This stream contains minute silt and clay particles that are difficult to separate with a wastewater decanter. A high-speed disc centrifuge has sufficient g-force to pull out these particles and clarify the water.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h3 class="font-heading text-xl font-bold text-navy mb-4">Separator for Industrial Oily Wastewater</h3>
  <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-3 gap-8 items-start my-8">
    <div class="bg-warm-white rounded-xl border border-gray-100 shadow-sm p-4">
      <figure>
        <img
          src="/images/wastewater-centrifuge/alfa-laval-decanter-centrifuge-NX416-300x300.webp"
          alt="Alfa Laval Decanter Centrifuge for Vegetable Processing Wastewater"
          width="300"
          height="300"
          loading="lazy"
          class="img-cap-300"
        />
        <figcaption>Alfa Laval NX-416 3-Phase Separator</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
    <div class="md:col-span-2 prose-dolphin">
      <p>
        Industrial wastewater often carries oil in addition to sludge. This oily water is challenging because it cannot be disposed of in standard drains. A three-phase industrial centrifuge separates the oil from the water, making it disposable.
      </p>
      <p>
        Industrial cleaning fluid containing shop-floor sludge and oil from the manufacturing run-off is ideal for separation with a liquid-liquid-solid separator.
      </p>
      <p>
        Where the separated oil phase is worth recovering rather than paying to dispose of, the same three-phase machine is packaged as a <a href="/waste-oil-centrifuge/">waste oil centrifuge system</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h3 class="font-heading text-xl font-bold text-navy mb-4">Oilfield Produced Water</h3>
  <p>
    Produced water is the water that comes up the well with the crude, and it arrives carrying dispersed oil, emulsified oil, and formation solids. Gravity separators and hydrocyclones take out the bulk, but the oil-in-water number at the outlet is often still too high for reinjection or discharge, and the remaining oil is fine enough that it will not settle in any practical residence time.
  </p>
  <p>
    A centrifuge finishes the job. Dolphin builds produced water systems on remanufactured Alfa Laval machines: an <strong><a href="/alfa-laval-nx-418-decanter-centrifuge/">Alfa Laval NX 418</a></strong> or <strong>NX 414</strong> decanter first where the solids loading is heavy, then a three-phase disc stack centrifuge such as the <strong>Alfa Laval MOPX 213</strong> or <strong>WHPX 510</strong> to pull the residual oil out of the water and send a recovered oil stream back to the tank. The same two-stage arrangement handles tank bottoms, frac flowback, and slop oil. Sizing depends on the oil cut, the solids content, and how tight the discharge limit is, so send a sample and we will run it before quoting.
  </p>

  <h2 id="benefits">Benefits of Centrifuges for Wastewater</h2>

  <p>
    Centrifuge offers many benefits compared to other dewatering and separation technologies. A brief description of some of the advantages follows.
  </p>

  <h3>Reduced Transportation and Disposal Expenses</h3>

  <p>
    Centrifuges produce dry sludge cake, significantly reducing transportation costs (up to 50%). Dry sludge also reduces disposal costs because the disposal volume is a fraction of the wet sludge.
  </p>

  <h3>Equipment Durability and Longevity</h3>

  <p>
    Industrial decanters are heavy-duty machines that have long service lives. Their material of construction (Stainless Steel) provides corrosion resistance in corrosive processing applications.
  </p>
  <p>
    These centrifuges have superior erosion protection in critical areas for enhanced wear protection. It is not uncommon for these dewatering machines to operate for decades without needing replacement or even significant rebuilds.
  </p>

  <h3>Low Operating Expense</h3>

  <p>
    Unlike belt and filter presses, centrifuges do not use filter media, significantly benefiting these machines' users. Also, this eliminates the labor cost related to media replacement.
  </p>
  <p>
    The only operational expense associated with centrifuges is maintenance. The only parts that need occasional replacement are the bearings because a decanter has fewer moving parts than filter presses.
  </p>

  <h3>Minimized Operator Requirements</h3>

  <p>
    Most sludge-thickening and dewatering centrifuges are fully automatic. The control systems have built-in sensors with self-correcting features to allow operator-free processing.
  </p>
  <p>
    Other methods mentioned above need manual service to replace media or perform other periodic functions.
  </p>

  <h3>Compact Size for Processing Capacity</h3>

  <p>
    Centrifuges are compact when compared to other dewatering equipment. For equivalent capacity, a decanter footprint is often a fraction of other comparable machines. This small size makes them a viable option for wastewater de-sludging in confined areas in treatment plants.
  </p>
  <p>
    The centrifuge equipment's compact size means that its location can be changed or moved to the worksite for ease of use and flexibility. A typical NX-314 decanter measures just 3 feet by 8 feet by 4 feet high while processing up to 40 GPM of waste water at 5% sludge.
  </p>

  <h2 id="specifications">Wastewater Centrifuge Specifications and Sizing</h2>

  <p class="mb-8">
    Centrifuge specifications and sizing depend highly on the manufacturer and the customer’s capacity throughput requirements. Listed below are some specific technical details and sizes of the commonly used dewatering separators from Alfa Laval.
  </p>

  <h3 id="sizing-example" class="font-heading text-xl font-bold text-navy mb-4">Selecting a Decanter for a Wastewater Job</h3>

  <p>Selection of a decanter for a wastewater job is based primarily on the percentage of solids and the desired actual feed rate into the decanter. Once that is established, decanters have published data sheets which show the range of flow capacities, and that narrows it down to a specific decanter.</p>

  <p class="mb-8">For example, a customer calls and says they have a wastewater stream from an industrial washing system with up to 10% sludge, and they desire a real flow rate on the inlet side of about 25 gallons per minute. We would advise that they go for the Alfa Laval NX-314 decanter. Real-world experience shows us that at 25 GPM, this decanter can do a great job of separating about 10% of sludge out of wastewater.</p>

